## Abstract

Agents lose the plot. The field has converged on this — goal drift is now a named, measured
failure mode with formal metrics and a growing literature — but the tooling has converged on
two answers that are both post-hoc or both about policy: statistical drift detection over
batches of traces, and declarative guardrails that decide whether a tool call is permitted.
Neither answers, during a run, the question an agent's operator actually has: *is this still
the errand we started?*

We describe an instrument that answers it deterministically, in-loop, with no model call and
no embedding: it freezes the goal as stated at the first step, measures each subsequent
state against that frozen ground by set overlap, and blocks the next tool call when the
displacement crosses a published threshold. We report {readings} readings across {runs} runs
and one week of continuous production use on real software work.

Most of what we report is negative and, we think, more useful than a positive result would
be. The instrument fires on {fire_rate}% of readings, {goaldrift_share}% of those fires are
the same verdict, and of the {clear} fires an independent rule can call cleanly,
{precision}% were warranted. The dominant failure is not sensitivity or calibration. It is
that **a legitimate redirect and a genuine drift are the same measurement**, and the
affordance built to separate them — a slot for declaring a sub-goal's parent — was used in
{parent_pct}% of readings and fired {excursions} times. We trace that non-adoption to a
specific mechanism, show it is not an awareness problem, and argue it generalizes to any
frozen-reference design.

One result is positive, and it comes from declining to resolve that ambiguity at all.
Conditioning each reading on **how long the agent had run since a person last spoke** —
joinable because every user message is a timestamped record — gives a monotone rise in
goal-drift with unattended runtime, {speech_lo}% inside a minute against {speech_hi}% past
half an hour, z = {speech_z} between the two best-powered bands. The ambiguity that makes
{unclear} fires unlabellable can be designed around even where it cannot be solved.

