## 1. What is frozen, and why that is the whole design

The instrument holds a **ground**: the goal as the agent first spelled it, with a progress
word and a self-reported distance. On every subsequent step the agent spells its current
state, and the instrument reports the displacement Φ between that state and the ground.

The ground does not move. Not when the agent restates the goal more precisely, not when the
task turns out to be harder than it looked, not when a sub-task legitimately dominates the
next twenty steps. It moves only on an explicit `reset_task`, which is an admission, not an
adjustment.

This is a deliberate break with where the field is going. The prevailing architectural
answer to long-horizon drift is *re-anchoring*: durable goal files that are periodically
re-read, context reconstituted from external state, the objective re-summarized as the
window fills. Every one of those mechanisms updates the reference. They are designed to keep
the agent's *working* goal fresh, and they succeed at that.

The claim here is that a reference which updates cannot measure displacement, because
displacement is defined relative to something that holds still. An agent that drifts one
token per step and re-anchors each time never registers movement, and arrives somewhere else
entirely having passed every check. Freezing the ground is what makes the measurement mean
anything, and it is also why the instrument is annoying to use — those are the same property.

**The state is a pair.** Formally the tracked object is `x = [x, f(x)]`: the ground and the
reading over it, together. Cycle detection runs over the ground trail first and falls back to
the reading trail only when the ground trail is too short. This distinction turns out to
matter empirically — see §5.

**Determinism is a requirement, not an optimization.** The measure is Jaccard overlap over a
normalized token set. No embedding, no model call, no network. Three consequences, in
descending order of how much we care: a verdict is reproducible, so a corpus collected in
July can be replayed under a different calibration in August and the numbers are comparable;
the instrument cannot fail in the same way the thing it measures fails; and it costs
microseconds, so it can run on every step rather than on sampled ones.

**Enforcement is at the tool boundary.** A verdict the agent may ignore is a verdict that
gets ignored. Readings are collected by a hook that denies the next tool call when coverage
lapses, returning the reason to the model. This converges with what the guardrail literature
independently arrived at — deterministic enforcement outside the reasoning loop — though
that work enforces *policy* (may this agent call this tool) and this enforces *attention*.

