## 4. Precision, and the {unclear} fires nobody can call

Fires were labelled by rule rather than by judgement. An agent grading the instrument that
judged it, on its own runs, from memory, is the weakest evidence available, and the corpus
records `by` on every label precisely so that kind can be discounted. The rule instead asks
what the run itself did afterwards: did it return to the ground the fire was measured
against (→ the fire was **useful**), or carry on and close distance on the new goal (→ the
fire was a **false alarm**)?

Of {fires} fires, {labelled} are labelled ({labelled_pct}%):

| outcome | n |
|---|---|
| useful | {useful} |
| false alarm | {false} |
| **the rule declined** | **{unclear}** |

**Precision is {useful}/{clear} = {precision}%** on fires with a clear call. We report it
because it is what the data says, and we do not think it should be read as "the instrument
is {precision}% accurate". {unclear_pct}% of labelled fires got no call at all, and the
reason those {unclear} are unclear is the actual finding of this paper.

