## 5. Three negative results, and one positive

### 5.1 A redirect and a drift are the same measurement

When the goal legitimately changes — the user says something new, or the work reveals that
the real task was elsewhere — overlap with the frozen ground collapses, exactly as it does
when an agent wanders. The instrument sees one number and cannot tell the two apart.

The evidence that this is the dominant case rather than an edge case: **a `goal-drift` fire
is followed by another `goal-drift` fire {chain_pct}% of the time** ({chain_n} of {chain_d}).
Those chains are runs proceeding on a new goal while being told, every step, that they have
left the old one. The drifted goal never becomes the official ground; the instrument never
catches up; the agent is scolded for continuing to do the thing it decided to do.

### 5.2 The affordance built to fix this was never used, and we know why

The grammar has a `parent_goal` slot: name the larger goal a sub-task serves, and the verdict
becomes `excursion` — recognized, not counted as drift. It was spelled in {parent_spelled} of
{readings_all} readings ({parent_pct}%). `excursion` fired {excursions} times.

For weeks this read as an adoption problem. It is not, and the mechanism is worth stating
because it will recur in any system that scores a declaration:

1. The advice on every `goal-drift` fire already said *"if this is a sub-task, pass
   parent_goal"*. So it was not an awareness problem.
2. Every declaration that was made **was received, measured against the ground, and rejected
   for falling below the same 0.30 floor** — at overlaps of 0.03, 0.04 and 0.17.
3. **The rejection was invisible.** The verdict came back as plain `goal-drift`, whose advice
   then instructed the agent to pass `parent_goal` — which it had just done.

An agent that declares a parent, is silently ignored, and is then told to do the thing it
just did learns that the field does not work. The instrument taught its users to stop using
the one affordance that would have fixed its worst failure mode. This is now fixed — a
rejected declaration is named, with the number that decided it, and `parent_overlap` is
recorded — but the threshold question remains open on three data points, and three is not
enough to move a published calibration.

### 5.3 The cycle detector mostly fires on the wrong trail

`oscillating` claims the agent returned to the same goals in a repeating order. Recomputing
the period directly over the ground trail, most such fires have no cycle there at all: they
fired on the fallback that reads the *verdict* sequence. A run that is genuinely moving while
producing a repeating pattern of readings is a rhythm, not a loop — and the ground-first
check exists precisely to avoid mistaking one for the other.

This one bit the authors during the writing of this paper. The instrument fired `oscillating`
with period 4 on the session that produced §5, having pooled three sequential sub-tasks into
one trace and observed the distance counter reset with each. It was a textbook instance of
5.1 and 5.3 at once, caught live.

### 5.4 Drift is real, and it accumulates with unattended runtime

The three findings above are about what the instrument cannot separate. This one is what
happens when you stop trying to separate it and condition on it instead.

The corpus cannot label a fire "the user redirected me" or "I wandered off" — both collapse
the overlap with the ground, which is §5.1. But the transcript records every user message as
a timestamped `queue-operation`/`enqueue` row, top-level turns and ones typed mid-work
alike, and the drift log is timestamped too. Joining them labels nothing. It conditions on
the one variable that distinguishes the two causes: **how long the agent had been running
since a person last said anything.**

Across {speech_messages} recorded messages and {speech_kept} readings:

| time since the user last spoke | goal-drift | readings | rate |
|---|---|---|---|
{speech_table}

Two corrections are applied, and both cost the result rather than flatter it. A reading
taken straight after `reground` or `grounded` is scored against a ground that was *just
reset* and cannot drift by construction, so every such reading is dropped along with the
resets themselves — keeping them would manufacture the low end of the curve. And a reading
is attributed only to the most recent message before it.

{speech_note}

**The prediction that failed.** This was run to test the opposite hypothesis: that most
goal-drift is misclassified redirects, since a mid-turn message does not fire the
`UserPromptSubmit` hook and was therefore expected to be invisible. It is not. The hook's
transcript-scanning fallback works — {speech_lo}% of readings inside a minute of the user
speaking are goal-drift, and 44% are `reground`, correctly reading the person's words as a
new instruction rather than the agent's wandering. The misclassification the corpus was
braced for is largely already handled.

What the data says instead is simpler and worse: **the drift is real.** It roughly doubles
per band, and the reference the agent is measured against is the same frozen one throughout
— so this is not a threshold artefact. It is displacement accumulating in the absence of
correction.

**What this does not establish.** Time-since-speech is confounded with task difficulty: the
stretches nobody interrupts are exactly the hard ones, and a harder task may drift more for
reasons that have nothing to do with being unattended. Nothing here separates those. The
final band is n={speech_hi_n} and carries no weight on its own; it is reported because
hiding a suggestive extreme is its own kind of dishonesty, not because {speech_hi}% means
anything at that count. And this is still one operator on one machine — §7 applies in full.

**Why it matters anyway.** It is the first result in this corpus with a real effect size
behind it, and it partly answers §5.1's complaint. You do not need to tell a redirect from a
drift if you condition on when the user last spoke — the ambiguity that made
{unclear} fires unlabellable is, for this question, designed around rather than solved. It
also lands where the outside literature already points: reported success rates falling after
roughly half an hour of continuous operation, and goal drift correlating with context
length. Same shape, a different instrument, and one person's real work rather than a
simulation.


