## 7. What this study cannot support

**Every label is ours.** The rules are reproducible and wrong in the same direction every
time, which is better than an opinion, and is still not independent. `replay.py` exists to
score the grammar against foreign traces, and found on inspection that the two public suites
supply per-*run* labels only: the goal-drift evaluations compute their metrics against a
baseline run and interrogate the model at the end, and the adversarial-pressure probes
measure system-prompt violation under manipulation, which is a different phenomenon. So the
external claim available is per-run agreement, and per-step precision remains internal.

**One agent, effectively.** Readings by agent:

| agent | readings | fires |
|---|---|---|
{agent_table}

The second-largest producer is {agent_second}. Any comparison across agents from this corpus
is underpowered to the point of meaninglessness, and an earlier draft of this work quoted a
p-value that turned out to compare an agent's readings against an era they predated.

**One operator, one machine, one week.** Selection effects on task type, working style and
tolerance for interruption are total and unmeasured.

**The instrument is not blind to its own subject.** These readings were produced while
building the instrument. The corpus's most-represented task is its own construction.

---

## 8. What we think is true

The frozen ground is the right primitive, and the evidence for it here is indirect: the
instrument's failures are all failures of *interpretation* — telling a redirect from a drift —
and none are failures of *measurement*. Φ was never wrong about displacement. It was
repeatedly wrong about what displacement meant.

That points somewhere specific. The missing element is not a better threshold, a smarter
detector, or a model in the loop. It is a way for the subject to say **"the errand changed,
and here is what it changed to"** that the instrument will actually honour — and the
{parent_pct}% adoption of exactly such a slot, for the mechanical reason in §5.2, is the most
transferable thing in this paper. Build the affordance, score the declaration, and then make
the score *visible*, or you will train your users out of the feature and read the result as
apathy.

